$fieldc2 = stripslashes('<p data-end=\"156\" data-start=\"112\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size:22px;\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"><strong data-end=\"156\" data-start=\"115\">Pressure Altitude vs Density Altitude</strong></span></span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<h2 data-end=\"156\" data-start=\"112\"><strong>1️⃣ Prssure Altitude<br />
2️⃣ Density Altitude<br />
3️⃣ Key Differences<br />
4️⃣ Calculating Zp Vs Zd</strong><br />
&nbsp;</h2>

<h2 data-end=\"192\" data-start=\"158\"><strong data-end=\"192\" data-start=\"162\">1️⃣ Pressure Altitude (PA)</strong></h2>

<p data-end=\"365\" data-start=\"194\"><strong data-end=\"209\" data-start=\"194\">Definition:</strong> The altitude the aircraft “indicates” if the atmospheric pressure at the aircraft’s level were normalized to standard pressure (1013.25 hPa or 29.92 inHg).</p>

<p data-end=\"388\" data-start=\"367\"><strong data-end=\"386\" data-start=\"367\">How to find it:</strong></p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"427\" data-start=\"391\">Set the altimeter to QNH 1013 hPa.</li>
	<li data-end=\"477\" data-start=\"430\">The altimeter then shows the pressure altitude.</li>
</ul>

<p data-end=\"491\" data-start=\"479\"><strong data-end=\"489\" data-start=\"479\">Usage:</strong></p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"545\" data-start=\"494\">Crucial for flight plans and aircraft separation.</li>
	<li data-end=\"630\" data-start=\"548\">Also used to calculate theoretical aircraft performance under standard conditions.</li>
</ul>

<p data-end=\"653\" data-start=\"632\"><strong data-end=\"651\" data-start=\"632\">Characteristic:</strong></p>

<ul data-end=\"698\" data-start=\"654\">
	<li data-end=\"698\" data-start=\"654\">
	<p data-end=\"698\" data-start=\"656\">Depends only on pressure, not temperature.<br />
	&nbsp;</p>
	</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-end=\"738\" data-start=\"705\"><strong data-end=\"738\" data-start=\"709\">2️⃣ Density Altitude (DA)</strong></h2>

<p data-end=\"841\" data-start=\"740\"><strong data-end=\"755\" data-start=\"740\">Definition:</strong> Altitude corrected for temperature and humidity that reflects the actual air density.</p>

<p data-end=\"866\" data-start=\"843\"><strong data-end=\"864\" data-start=\"843\">How to calculate:</strong></p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"904\" data-start=\"869\">Start from the pressure altitude.</li>
	<li data-end=\"1004\" data-start=\"907\">Correct for temperature: the warmer it is, the higher the density altitude (air is less dense).</li>
	<li data-end=\"1070\" data-start=\"1007\">Humidity also plays a role: humid air → less dense → higher DA.</li>
</ul>

<p data-end=\"1084\" data-start=\"1072\"><strong data-end=\"1082\" data-start=\"1072\">Usage:</strong></p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"1147\" data-start=\"1087\">Influences engine performance, lift, and takeoff distance.</li>
	<li data-end=\"1203\" data-start=\"1150\">Higher density altitude → lower aircraft performance.</li>
</ul>

<p data-end=\"1226\" data-start=\"1205\"><strong data-end=\"1224\" data-start=\"1205\">Characteristic:</strong></p>

<ul data-end=\"1316\" data-start=\"1227\">
	<li data-end=\"1316\" data-start=\"1227\">
	<p data-end=\"1316\" data-start=\"1229\">Depends on pressure and temperature, so it better reflects actual aircraft performance.<br />
	&nbsp;</p>
	</li>
</ul>

<h3 data-end=\"1349\" data-start=\"1323\"><strong data-end=\"1349\" data-start=\"1327\">3️⃣ Key Differences</strong></h3>

<table class=\"w-fit min-w-(--thread-content-width)\" data-end=\"2086\" data-start=\"1351\">
	<thead data-end=\"1471\" data-start=\"1351\">
		<tr data-end=\"1471\" data-start=\"1351\">
			<th data-col-size=\"sm\" data-end=\"1374\" data-start=\"1351\">Characteristic</th>
			<th data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1416\" data-start=\"1374\">Pressure Altitude (PA)</th>
			<th data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1471\" data-start=\"1416\">Density Altitude (DA)</th>
		</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody data-end=\"2086\" data-start=\"1590\">
		<tr data-end=\"1707\" data-start=\"1590\">
			<td data-col-size=\"sm\" data-end=\"1612\" data-start=\"1590\">Depends on</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1654\" data-start=\"1612\">Pressure only</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1707\" data-start=\"1654\">Pressure + Temperature (+ Humidity)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr data-end=\"1839\" data-start=\"1708\">
			<td data-col-size=\"sm\" data-end=\"1730\" data-start=\"1708\">Indicates</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1786\" data-start=\"1730\">Position on the planet according to standard pressure</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1839\" data-start=\"1786\">Actual air density affecting performance</td>
		</tr>
		<tr data-end=\"1968\" data-start=\"1840\">
			<td data-col-size=\"sm\" data-end=\"1862\" data-start=\"1840\">Main use</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1904\" data-start=\"1862\">Air traffic separation, navigation</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"1968\" data-start=\"1904\">Engine and aerodynamic performance, takeoff/landing distance</td>
		</tr>
		<tr data-end=\"2086\" data-start=\"1969\">
			<td data-col-size=\"sm\" data-end=\"1991\" data-start=\"1969\">Typical measurement</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"2033\" data-start=\"1991\">Altimeter set to 1013 hPa</td>
			<td data-col-size=\"md\" data-end=\"2086\" data-start=\"2033\">Calculated from PA + temperature correction</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

<p data-end=\"2121\" data-start=\"2088\"><br />
💡 <strong data-end=\"2119\" data-start=\"2091\">Practical aviation rule:</strong></p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"2176\" data-start=\"2124\">Higher temperature → DA &gt; PA → reduced performance</li>
	<li data-end=\"2229\" data-start=\"2179\">Lower temperature → DA &lt; PA → improved performance<br />
	&nbsp;</li>
</ul>

<h3 data-end=\"2268\" data-start=\"2236\"><strong data-end=\"2268\" data-start=\"2240\">4️⃣ Calculating Zp vs Zd</strong></h3>

<p>Formula to find Density Altitude (Zd) knowing Pressure Altitude (Zp) and Static Temperature (Ts):<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">Zd = Zp +/– 120 ft per degree + or - of standard temperature (T°std)</span></span></strong><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p data-end=\"2473\" data-start=\"2457\"><strong data-end=\"2471\" data-start=\"2457\">Example 1:</strong><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\"></span></span></p>

<p data-end=\"2473\" data-start=\"2457\"><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">Zp = 10 000 ft.<br />
Calculation of T°std at 10 000 ft : (at Zp : 0 ft +15°C) -2°c per 1000 ft) : +15° - ( 2° x 10) = -5°C<br />
<br />
Thus at Zp10 000 ft the Tstd is – 5°<br />
<br />
If the outside static temperature Ts is + 6°C we have an increase because Ts &gt; T°std:<br />
Zd =10 000 ft + (120 ft X 11) = 11320 ft.</span></span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p data-end=\"2707\" data-start=\"2691\"><strong data-end=\"2705\" data-start=\"2691\">Example 2:</strong></p>

<p data-end=\"2839\" data-start=\"2787\"><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">Zp = 30 000 ft ; T°std = - 45° if Ts = - 20°c in those conditions Ts &gt; of 25°c at T°std, thus + 120 ft for each degree above : 120 X 25<br />
Zd = 30 000 ft + (120 X 25) = 33 000 ft</span></span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p data-end=\"2863\" data-start=\"2841\"><strong data-end=\"2861\" data-start=\"2841\">Important Notes:</strong></p>

<p>- This formula is valid only if the actual Ts is known, as thermometer heating varies by type.<br />
- The temperature reading on a pitot-static thermometer depends on True Airspeed (TAS):<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<ul>
	<li>+ 1,9°c at 120 kt</li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 3°c at 150 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 5,3°c at 200 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 11,9°c at 300 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 21°c at 400 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 32,8°c at 500 kt</span></span><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\"></span></span></li>
</ul>

<p>- On a friction thermometer, only 84% of the heating is measured:<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<ul>
	<li>+ 1,6°c at 120 kt</li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 2.5°c at 150 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 4,5°c at 200 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+10°c at 300 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+17,6°c at 400 kt</span></span></li>
	<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000;\">+ 27,5°c at 500 kt</span></span></li>
</ul>

<p>For mental calculations in flight, rounding to the nearest value is recommended.</p>

<p></p>

<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note that on modern aircraft, the temperature correction is applied by the onboard computers, depending on Mach.<br data-end=\"208\" data-start=\"205\" />
On the Airbus A300 (and on the A320 and later), the Total Air Temperature (TAT) is corrected by two modules (computerized). The first module receives the Mach information and provides the TAS (True Air Speed, Vp in French!), while the second module corrects the TAT into SAT (Static Air Temperature).<br data-end=\"511\" data-is-only-node=\"\" data-start=\"508\" />
Thus, on the A300, the SAT indicator located above the landing gear position indicator shows a direct reading of Ts (without correction).<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p>Roland IT022</p>
